otes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Mississippi remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, having backed the GOP by double digits in recent cycles. The state's partisan lean of roughly eleven points Republican sets the starting point for this open-seat contest, with no incumbent on the ballot after the term-limited president leaves office.\n\nNo public polls of Mississippi, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamental lean. National headlines in 2026 focus on the emerging primary fields rather than the Mississippi general election, offering little race-specific signal. The expectation is a Republican win in the low double digits, with meaningful uncertainty given the absence of direct measurement. A strong national wave for either party, or early state polling that diverges from the historical lean, would be the most likely reasons to revise.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1069, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1631, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1578, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1586, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1588, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.158, "margin": -11.0}]}